ficient because they don't  really change the fabric of society they  don't move towards a better state that  human beings are capable of Martin  Luther King doesn't just want to you  know put everybody down at the same low  level nor does he want it just to be you  know exactly the same all across in some  sort of middle class think he wants to  see a full realization of human  personality of human being of what it is  that our human nature affords us and  because he thinks of human beings as  primarily interrelation alanda beings  that has to happen within the framework  of society in all the little parts of  society like you know institutions or  organizations or government so it's not  just going to be equal treatment and  it's not just going to be things like  reparations I'm not going to cross that  out because that could be part of it  well what goes beyond that one of the  most robust clues about this is Kings  reference to the American dream and you  see this a bit more in the I have a  dream speech so I'm going to skip the  sections where he talks about this in  the letter from a Birmingham jail and  just you know focus on this so early on  in the eye of a dream speech he's  talking about the condition of African  Americans a hundred years more than a  hundred years after emancipation and he  said this you know there's this  declaration of emancipation raised a lot  of hopes those hopes have not been  realized what are were those hopes  raised so he says in a sense we've come  to our nation's capital to cash a check  now what does that mean the cash a check  that makes you probably think of  reparations it's not a monetary check  it's a check in terms of what it is that  America has an ideal and  nation promises to its citizens and this  is enshrined in you know the what we  call the organic law which includes the  Declaration of Independence and the  Constitution of the United States so he  says when the architects of our republic  wrote The Magnificent words of the  Constitution the Declaration of  Independence they were signing a  promissory note to which every American  was to fall heir this note was the  promise that all men yes black men as  well as white men would be guaranteed  the unalienable rights of life liberty  and the pursuit of happiness and then  you'll go on and say look it's pretty  clear that America has defaulted on this  promise so far that they've actually  issued a check that had insufficient  funds and you know you go to the bank  and you try to deposit in your quite  embarrassed when that happens when  african-americans try to cash in that  check oftentimes it led to much more  than embarrassment it led to things like  like you know terrorism and lynchings to  the whole segregation regime so he's  saying that the promise of America was  actually to provide all of its citizens  irrespective of color of race and  ethnicity with the same conditions the  same possibility to pursue the same  ideals the pursuit of happiness the same  protections of life and liberty and even  you know proper you ought to be rolled  in there if we want to get John Locke  yet about it so Martin Luther King is  going to frame this this long-standing  promise in terms of realizing what the  American dream was supposed to provide  to all of its citizens irrespective of  the sort of things that set them apart  for instance race ethnicity the sort of  things that allow people to be  discriminated against and just as a side  note Alexis de Tocqueville at this  French theorist who spent a lot of time  traveling around America observing  America and actually wrote a book called  democracy in America one of the things  that he noticed in 1800s and he was a  big supporter of America  and the whole you know notion of the  kind of new society that was being built  there one of the things that he called  attention to was precisely the fact that  white Americans had done a terrible job  in realizing any of this sort of  goodness any of this sort of justice any  sort of opportunities for two groups  were for the African Americans and for  Native Americans King here is primarily  talking about african-americans but this  would apply to any sort of discriminated  against class that this realization of  the American Dream is supposed to afford  you might say not only equal protection  Adi not only equal opportunity but  something more substantive so that's  going to provide him with a very  powerful metaphor that he's going to use  here let's look at the next issue he  talks about forces within the black  community that want to have segregation  in one way in which we're not segregated  separation sorry one way in which one  could think about racial justice would  be that the best way to attain it would  be to give african-americans or any  other minority their own place over here  let them govern themselves and then have  White's over here and there are some  some you know within the  african-american community at that time  who are saying look that's the best that  you're going to get you're not going to  get any justice in an integrated society  because somebody's always going to be on  top and somebody's always going to be on  bottom King says no that that that is  not what we're aiming for that's not  going to be a satisfactory resolution  it's going to have to be an integrated  society where blacks and whites and  everybody else can live next to each  other can participate in the same  institutions can engage within the same  culture can you know have dialogue can  have you know businesses together we  could just go down the line for this  sort of thing that's what racial justice  is going to require for for Martin  Luther  he talks about this is a very nice way  to frame this as being a substantive and  positive peace as opposed to a kind of  fake piece or a negative piece you know  if you just give everybody the same  thing and you don't really treat them as  if they matter or they only 