etending like  everything is going to get better over  time I'm happy to be an extremist for  love of truth and goodness and in the  transition to racial justice that would  be the sort of thing that would be going  on as a matter of fact what we're  calling extremism in this framework  wouldn't really be extreme it would be  the norm people would treat each other  in more loving ways people would respect  truth people would try to enact goodness  towards each other rather than that you  know the opposites finally he's got a  another great phrase he talks about a  beautiful symphony of brotherhood and if  you linger over this metaphor for a  moment what does a symphony like if you  have ever listened to classical music  it's really remarkable when you pay  attention to what's going on within a  symphony you have all these different  instruments and they're playing  different things but they somehow come  together even symphonies that use  dissonance you know really modern stuff  like Debussy or prokofiev or  Shostakovich people like that everything  has been arranged in such a way that the  musicians are able to harmonize and  synchronize with each other so that  their individual actions are not  impeding the other person's individual  actions or harming them but they're  actually building something that's  greater than themselves Martin Luther  King is not thinking of this in musical  terms he's thinking of this in moral  terms in terms of what he's calling here  Brotherhood or that you know treating  other people as again human persons as  treating treating them as if they matter  as if what they're trying to do the  things that they develop into can fit in  with other people without without  this is an ideal this is what he is  trying to strive for he's not saying  that this will be completely realized in  his lifetime it certainly wasn't a  hundred years from then probably won't  be even perhaps a thousand years but  these are ideals that allow him to have  something to shoot for now one question  that's really important that remains is  is this a society in which everybody  gets a gold star in which you know if to  respect personality means we have to you  know care about everybody else's  self-esteem all the time and pretend  like everybody's a winner no actually  that would be to go against what he's  talking about in terms of justice you  know go certainly go against truth if  you're not good at mathematics because  you didn't put in the work to study and  didn't do the homework you shouldn't get  the same grade as the person who  actually improved themselves so you know  ways got that line in there about I have  a dream a lot of people i think don't  actually consider they don't listen to  the first part of the line and they  don't think about the second part when  he says i have a dream that my four  little children will one day live in a  nation where they will not be judged by  the color of their skin there's the  first part but by the content of their  character to judge somebody on the  content of their character means to  actually say you're a good person or  you're a bad person and to be able to  provide reasons why to be able to say  well you know you're you steal from  everybody all the time and we've SAT you  down and showed you that it's hurting  other people and you say I don't care I  want the stuff that would be a bad  character and one could legitimately say  you're not as good of a person as this  person over here or keeps their hands  off of other people's stuff it actually  helps them out on the weekends you know  improving their their their places a lot  of people like I said just just focus on  the you know not being judged on the  color of skin  the not judgment the equalising  everything and they don't focus on the  fact that martin luther king is a very  strong advocate for judging on the  content of character one of the main  problems with segregation as he's  pointing it out is the detrimental  damaging effects that it has on the  human character on human personality so  in a certain way to you know be judged  by the content of one's character is to  be treated as a full human being it's to  be treated as somebody who has the  possibility for realizing what humanity  is now you notice that I've got another  topic over here that's connected with  this issue of racial justice King will  talk about what you know this this  notion of mutuality and he talks about a  mutuality of justice and injustice what  is mutuality mean it means that were not  all alone that we can't be completely  isolated from each other he says that  all communities and States so that is  all local communities every you know  sort of organization and every nation is  interrelated with others and you could  say well maybe that's a modern  phenomenon may be king is only talking  about the modern world in which he's  he's living but that is certainly the  case for the world that we live in today  even states that attempt to you know  wall themselves off like for example  North Korea they have refugees flowing  over into China that the Chinese have to  figure out what to do with you know you  can get information in and out there is  an inter connection there is a smaller  world that we all live in he also says  that injustice anywhere is a threat to  justice everywhere this is a stronger  statement to say that all the nations  all the communities are connected with  each other is not yet to say that what's  going on over there actually affects us  over here and this is not talking about  direct effect at this point it's talking  about indirect effect about you know  posing a threat so injustice  anywhere is a threat to justice  everywhere else the degree of justice  that has been realized communities  nations that haven't quite got it  together haven't moved towards through  social progress towards a more equitable  situation are not just you know dragging  the rest behind they could become the  norm like King has said you know in  other places there is no historical  inevitability to civil rights being  granted it requires good people to  actually put themselves at risk to you  know put their action out there in the  world for things like that to happen he  says oppressors do not give up their  power willingly it's only when people  stand up for for themselves or stand up  for others who are experiencing  injustice somewhere else which is the  case that's why he's writing a letter  from a Birmingham jail he saying to  those who are criticizing is actually  worth worth reading directly he says I  am in Birmingham which is not where he  lives it's a different community because  injustice is here he says I can add sit  by idly in Atlanta and not be concerned  about what happens in Birmingham  injustice anywhere is a threat to  justice everywhere we are caught in an  inescapable network of mutuality tied in  a single garment of destiny whatever  affects one directly affects all  indirectly and so he draws this  conclusion that we cannot afford to live  with this narrow provincial outside  agitator idea anyone who lives inside  the United States can never be  considered an outsider anywhere in its  bounds so if injustice is taking place  in Chicago that affects you and me even  if we're not living in Chicago if  injustice is taking effect in a very  different way in Texas then that affects  all of us of course it affects those who  are directly impacted by it more but  indirectly it affects all of us we all  have a stake in a few things so the  freedoms of people are connected  together in fact in the eye of a dream  speech he's lauding he's  raising the people who join with the  civil rights struggle precisely for  realizing that their freedom which they  think it you know they couldn't just  think is just their own freedom like the  white moderates do is in fact connected  with the freedom of those who are  suffering one way to realize this is to  try 